---
/**
 * K Culture Wire — **줄어든 것이 아니라 옮겨 갔다.** (`/where-it-moved`)
 *
 * ── ⛔ 이 지면이 지키는 것 ────────────────────────────────────
 * ⛔ 몫만 내지 않는다. **자리 수와 서로 다른 작품 수를 옆에 같이** 낸다 —
 *    몫이 준 것이 「한국 것이 줄어서」인지 「남의 것이 늘어서」인지 몫만으로는 못 가른다.
 * ⛔ 「인기가 식었다」라 안 쓴다. 우리가 본 것은 차트 자리뿐이다.
 * ⛔ 줄세우지 않는다. 늘어난 쪽과 줄어든 쪽을 **함께** 내고, 93곳을 가나다순으로도 낸다.
 * ⛔ 온전한 해만 견준다 — 2021 은 7월부터, 2026 은 7월까지라 반쪽이다.
 * ⛔ 광고 자리를 만들지 않았다. Riot Production(App 866800) 승인 전이다.
 * ⚠ 수를 손으로 적지 않는다. 전부 `wikitip-where-it-moved.json` 에서 읽는다.
 */
import WikiTip from '../../layouts/WikiTip.astro';
import KcwRelatedArticles from '../../components/KcwRelatedArticles.astro';
import data from '../../data/wikitip-where-it-moved.json';

const TITLE = 'Korean titles lost Asia’s charts and gained Latin America’s';
const 일본 = data.topFallers[0];
const 위 = data.topRisers[0];
const DESC =
  `Between ${data.beforeYear} and ${data.afterYear}, Korean titles lost chart share in all `
  + `${data.asianTen.markets} of the Asian markets we track — a mean of ${data.asianTen.meanChangePp} points — `
  + `while rising in ${data.elsewhere.roseCount} of the other ${data.elsewhere.markets}. The largest gains are in Latin America.`;

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Dataset',
  name: TITLE,
  description: DESC,
  temporalCoverage: `${data.beforeYear}/${data.afterYear}`,
  creator: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'K Culture Wire' },
  isBasedOn: ['https://www.netflix.com/tudum/top10', 'https://www.wikidata.org'],
};

/* ⛔ 앞칸(`---` 사이)은 **타입스크립트 자리**다. 여기에 JSX 를 쓰면 빌드가 선다 —
   2026-08-09 05:2x 에 `const 줄 = (x) => (<tr …>)` 로 썼다가 PARSE_ERROR 를 봤다.
   줄 만드는 것은 본문에서 한다. 여기서는 **값만** 다듬는다. */
const 부호 = (v) => (v > 0 ? `+${v}` : `${v}`);
const 가나다 = [...data.all].sort((a, b) => a.name.localeCompare(b.name));
---

<WikiTip title={TITLE} description={DESC} jsonLd={jsonLd}
  canonical="https://www.kculturewire.com/where-it-moved">
  <h1>{TITLE}</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Korean titles held 7.7% of the world’s Netflix top 10 places over five years and that figure
    barely moved. Underneath it, the map did. Comparing two whole years — {data.beforeYear} against
    {' '}{data.afterYear}, {data.beforeWeeks} weeks each — Korean share fell in every Asian market we
    track and rose in {data.elsewhere.roseCount} of the other {data.elsewhere.markets}.
  </p>
  <p class="q">{data.question}</p>

  <section>
    <h2>Two groups, {data.markets} markets</h2>
    <p class="note lead-in"><b>How share is counted.</b> {data.unit}</p>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>&nbsp;</th><th>Markets</th><th>Mean change</th><th>Markets that rose</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr class="edge">
            <td class="nm">The ten Asian markets</td>
            <td>{data.asianTen.markets}</td>
            <td class="tot">{data.asianTen.meanChangePp}p</td>
            <td>{data.asianTen.roseCount}</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="nm">Everywhere else</td>
            <td>{data.elsewhere.markets}</td>
            <td class="tot">+{data.elsewhere.meanChangePp}p</td>
            <td>{data.elsewhere.roseCount}</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      <b>Not one Asian market rose.</b> {data.roseCount} markets of {data.markets} gained share and
      {' '}{data.fellCount} lost it, and every one of the losses that matters is in the same region.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Where it went</h2>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Market</th><th>{data.beforeYear}</th><th>{data.afterYear}</th><th>Change</th>
            <th>Korean places</th><th>Different titles</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {data.topRisers.map((x) => (
            <tr class={x.inAsianTen ? "edge" : ""}>
              <td class="nm">{x.name}</td>
              <td>{x.beforePc}%</td>
              <td>{x.afterPc}%</td>
              <td class="tot">{부호(x.changePp)}p</td>
              <td>{x.beforePlaces} → {x.afterPlaces}</td>
              <td>{x.beforeTitles} → {x.afterTitles}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      <b>It is not one title carrying a small market.</b> The number of different Korean titles
      charting roughly doubled in these places — {위.name} went from {위.beforeTitles} to
      {' '}{위.afterTitles}. A market that had a few Korean hits now has a Korean shelf.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>And where it left</h2>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Market</th><th>{data.beforeYear}</th><th>{data.afterYear}</th><th>Change</th>
            <th>Korean places</th><th>Different titles</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {data.topFallers.map((x) => (
            <tr class={x.inAsianTen ? "edge" : ""}>
              <td class="nm">{x.name}</td>
              <td>{x.beforePc}%</td>
              <td>{x.afterPc}%</td>
              <td class="tot">{부호(x.changePp)}p</td>
              <td>{x.beforePlaces} → {x.afterPlaces}</td>
              <td>{x.beforeTitles} → {x.afterTitles}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="warn">
      <b>{일본.name} is the steepest fall on this site.</b> {일본.beforePc}% to {일본.afterPc}% —
      {' '}{일본.beforePlaces} Korean places down to {일본.afterPlaces}, across {일본.beforeTitles}
      {' '}different titles down to {일본.afterTitles}. Both numbers moved together, so this is not a
      single franchise ending.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What a share cannot tell you</h2>
    <p class="warn">{data.cannotSeparate}</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Every market, in alphabetical order</h2>
    <p class="note lead-in">Sorted by name, not by size. Shaded rows are the ten Asian markets.</p>
    <div class="scroll-x tall">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Market</th><th>{data.beforeYear}</th><th>{data.afterYear}</th><th>Change</th>
            <th>Korean places</th><th>Different titles</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {가나다.map((x) => (
            <tr class={x.inAsianTen ? "edge" : ""}>
              <td class="nm">{x.name}</td>
              <td>{x.beforePc}%</td>
              <td>{x.afterPc}%</td>
              <td class="tot">{부호(x.changePp)}p</td>
              <td>{x.beforePlaces} → {x.afterPlaces}</td>
              <td>{x.beforeTitles} → {x.afterTitles}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <KcwRelatedArticles page="/where-it-moved" />

  <section class="src">
    <h2>Where these numbers come from, and what they cannot answer</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Netflix</b> — {data.source}</li>
      <li>
        <b>Only whole years are compared.</b> Our window opens in July 2021 and closes in July 2026,
        so those two years are partial and are left out of every figure here.
        {data.beforeYear} and {data.afterYear} have {data.beforeWeeks} and {data.afterWeeks} weeks.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>The denominator is counted, not assumed.</b> Each market gives out {data.slotsPerWeek}
        {' '}places a week, so {data.beforeYear} offered {data.beforeSlots.toLocaleString('en-US')} and
        {' '}{data.afterYear} offered {data.afterSlots.toLocaleString('en-US')}. We take the week count
        from the data rather than assuming 52, because 2023 has 53 weeks and a fixed number would have
        quietly mis-stated a year.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>A top 10 is a rank list, not a viewing count.</b> Nothing here measures hours, and a
        title outside a country’s top 10 is invisible at any level of watching.
      </li>
      <li><b>Russia is excluded</b> from every figure on this site.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">
      <a href="/world-share">The five-year world figure</a> ·{' '}
      <a href="/catalogue-depth">All markets side by side</a> ·{' '}
      <a href="/about">How we count</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</WikiTip>
